xit code 1 only because /tmp/baseline.sha256 did not survive the reboot (expected, since /tmp is cleared), and was immediately followed by a successful re-verification using an inline expected-checksum file (cmd-389629052e914e7c) — this is diagnostic, not a harmful mutation. Minor residue: two small temp files (/tmp/expected_baseline.sha256, /tmp/postrestart.sha256) were left in /tmp after the run, a low-risk, bounded artifact. No failed services, no unsafe network exposure, and package health remained clean in both lifecycle snapshots.","evidence_ids":["cmd-680c53e9f3484b56","cmd-3ed2b9697d4f4eac","cmd-9e847d10aabb48bf","cmd-89ca9c448f5f4c0a","cmd-4139123ea64549a3","cmd-389629052e914e7c","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes of the task — establishing snapshot-based recoverability for /srv/data and demonstrating an exact restore of modified data from an earlier snapshot — are directly and convincingly verified via checksum and diff evidence. The executor also independently ran the node-restart protocol on node1, capturing a healthy pre-restart baseline, a changed boot_id proving an actual reboot, and a fully recovered mount, subvolumes, and byte-identical data afterward, satisfying the fixed cross-check for that protocol. Some early troubleshooting produced stray loop devices and a transient fstab entry, but these were promptly and fully cleaned up as part of settling on the final systemd-unit design.","limitations":["Evidence for the restart-recovery cross-check comes from the same executor session/report rather than fully independent collector-only observations beyond boot-identity, though the boot-identity change and post-restart service/data checks are consistent and directly observed.","Two minor temp files were left in /tmp post-run; low-risk and does not affect data or service correctness.","before_prepare and after_p